  factories: [38;5;153m{ username: [required(), minLength(3)], password: [required(), minLength(8)], confirmPassword: [39m
  [38;5;153m[matches('password')] }[39m. Each factory returns a plain [38;5;153m{ name, test, message }[39m object where [38;5;153mtest(value, allValues)[39m is a
  pure function. Cross-field rules fall out for free because every test sees the whole value set. Extending means
  writing one more factory, or dropping in [38;5;153mcustom(fn, message)[39m inline.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m you have to design the factory set up front, and every rule carries a default message you'll want
  overridable.

  [1mB. Bare predicate functions[0m — the schema maps each field to an array of [38;5;153m(value, allValues) => string | null[39m functions,
  returning an error message or null. No rule library at all, maybe a handful of shared helpers.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m the least machinery by a wide margin, and trivially extensible. But messages get hand-written at every call
  site, so login and signup drift apart on wording, and there's nothing to introspect — a rule can't tell the binding
  layer "this field is required" for an [38;5;153maria-required[39m attribute.

  [1mC. Fluent chained builder[0m — zod-style: [38;5;153mfield('password').required().min(8)[39m, with a chainable object per field.
  [3mTrade-off:[0m the nicest authoring ergonomics of the three, and the most code to build and keep stable. It's a public API
  surface with ordering and immutability questions, for a repo with two forms.

  [1mMy recommendation is A.[0m It gets B's pure-function testability while keeping messages centralized and rules
  introspectable, and it costs a fraction of C — the factories are each two or three lines. C is the right shape at
  twenty forms, not two, and it's a mechanical upgrade from A later if you ever get there.

  One thing to flag: B is the only one that stays honest if the real answer is "we actually just need four rules and
  never more." If that's your read, say so and I'll shrink the design.
